<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaret, Maid of Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1283–<a href="/years/1290">1290</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Margaret of Scotland (1283–1290) · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ynglings">Ynglings</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaret, Maid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, was the daughter of King Eric II of Norway and the granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-iii-of-scotland">Alexander III of Scotland</a>, and was recognised as Queen of Scots after his death in 1286. The Treaty of Birgham of 1290 arranged her marriage to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england">Edward II of England</a>, but she died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> on her voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Her death left the succession open and led to Edward I's intervention.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Margaret was born at Tønsberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> between March and 9 April 1283, the only child of King Eric II of Norway and Margaret, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-iii-of-scotland">Alexander III of Scotland</a>, who died apparently from complications of childbirth. The infant was kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in the custody of Narve, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Bergen.</p><p>Alexander III's last surviving son died on 28 January 1284, and on 5 February the king had his earls, barons and clan chiefs swear at Scone to accept Margaret as his heir. Alexander set out on horseback in stormy weather on the evening of 18 March 1286 and was found dead with a broken neck the next day, and his posthumous child by Yolanda of Dreux was stillborn in November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Alexander's death, Scottish magnates chose six guardians to govern the country. Robert Bruce, 5th Lord of Annandale, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-balliol">John Balliol</a>, Lord of Galloway, were in dispute, and Bruce raised a rebellion with his son, the Earl of Carrick, which was defeated in early 1287. Because the situation was precarious, Eric was reluctant to send his three-year-old daughter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><p>In 1289 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> negotiated her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england">Edward of Caernarfon</a>, and the Treaty of Salisbury of 6 November 1289 asked Edward to act for his grandniece. The Treaty of Birgham, agreed on 18 July 1290, provided that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> would remain fully independent despite the union and that Margaret alone would be inaugurated at Scone.</p><p>Margaret sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in late summer 1290 on her father's ship, with Bishop Narve in her entourage. She fell ill, and the ship put in at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> about 23 September. She suffered for up to a week, from food poisoning or, less likely, motion sickness, and died between 26 and 29 September 1290 in the arms of Bishop Narve. Her coffin was taken to Bergen, where Eric had it opened to confirm her identity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Margaret was the last legitimate descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-lion">William the Lion</a>, and thirteen men claimed the succession, chiefly Bruce and Balliol. In 1301 a German woman impersonated her and was burned at the stake. Historians disagree whether she was a queen. She was never inaugurated, and Scots called her their lady far more often than their queen. Her biographer Archie Duncan argues that she was never queen of Scots, while Norman H. Reid holds that she was accepted as queen, though her reign never started. Modern writers nearly always call her queen, and reference books give 19 March 1286 as the start of her reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Margaret, Maid of Norway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The second Treaty of Birgham, under which <b>Margaret, Maid of Norway</b>, was to marry Edward of Caernarfon, son of Edward I of England, was ratified at Northampton.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-28">28 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tønsberg, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkwall">Kirkwall</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.27,10.41&amp;year=1287" aria-label="Tønsberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4769 171 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Margaret, Maid of Norway"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5219 441h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5219 441h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.27,10.41&amp;year=1287">What was here in 1287? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
